<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A triangulation of a polygon is a subdivision of it into triangles, using diagonals between its vertices. Two different triangulations of a polygon can be related by a sequence of flips: a flip replaces a diagonal by the unique other diagonal in the quadrilateral it defines.
In this paper, we study <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured triangulations and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips: we allocate <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colours to the triangles and flip diagonals only if the two triangles incident with it have the same colour, say <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The flip then changes to colour of the two triangles according to the colour <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> (reducing modulo <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>).
When using colours,
it is no longer true that any two triangulations can be linked by a sequence of (coloured) flips.
In this paper, we study the connected components of the coloured flip graphs of triangulations. The motivation for this is a result of
Gravier and Payan proving that the Four-Colour Theorem is equivalent to the connectedness of the flip graph of 2-coloured triangulations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This article is structured as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> contains the background on triangulated polygons and introduces coloured triangulations. Then it explains the link between coloured triangulations and the Four-Colour theorem. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we study the size and structure of the connected components of the coloured flip graph. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Observations and a conjecture ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> contains further observations and a conjecture.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we recall the notions of triangulations of convex polygons.
We write <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a convex polygon with <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Triangulation)<span id="S2.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">triangulation</em> of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivision of the polygon into triangles, using pairwise non-crossing diagonals.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Boundary segments are not considered to be diagonals.
Note that any triangulation of <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes the polygon into <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> triangles, using <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-3</annotation></semantics></math> diagonals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A triangulation given by <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-3</annotation></semantics></math> diagonals incident with a common vertex will be called a <span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fan triangulation</span>. An example of a fan triangulation of a hexagon
is in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In Example 2.2. ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="112.18" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 86.59 112.18" width="86.59"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,112.18) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(53.14,0) translate(0,21.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -9.84 88.58 L 31.5 62.99 L 31.5 7.87 L -9.84 -19.69 L -51.18 7.87 L -51.18 62.99 L -12.68 86.82"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -7.89 88.58 C -7.89 89.66 -8.76 90.54 -9.84 90.54 C -10.92 90.54 -11.8 89.66 -11.8 88.58 C -11.8 87.5 -10.92 86.63 -9.84 86.63 C -8.76 86.63 -7.89 87.5 -7.89 88.58 Z M -9.84 88.58"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M 33.45 62.99 C 33.45 64.07 32.58 64.95 31.5 64.95 C 30.42 64.95 29.54 64.07 29.54 62.99 C 29.54 61.91 30.42 61.04 31.5 61.04 C 32.58 61.04 33.45 61.91 33.45 62.99 Z M 31.5 62.99"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M 33.45 7.87 C 33.45 8.95 32.58 9.83 31.5 9.83 C 30.42 9.83 29.54 8.95 29.54 7.87 C 29.54 6.79 30.42 5.92 31.5 5.92 C 32.58 5.92 33.45 6.79 33.45 7.87 Z M 31.5 7.87"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -7.89 -19.69 C -7.89 -18.6 -8.76 -17.73 -9.84 -17.73 C -10.92 -17.73 -11.8 -18.6 -11.8 -19.69 C -11.8 -20.77 -10.92 -21.64 -9.84 -21.64 C -8.76 -21.64 -7.89 -20.77 -7.89 -19.69 Z M -9.84 -19.69"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -49.22 7.87 C -49.22 8.95 -50.1 9.83 -51.18 9.83 C -52.26 9.83 -53.14 8.95 -53.14 7.87 C -53.14 6.79 -52.26 5.92 -51.18 5.92 C -50.1 5.92 -49.22 6.79 -49.22 7.87 Z M -51.18 7.87"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -49.22 62.99 C -49.22 64.07 -50.1 64.95 -51.18 64.95 C -52.26 64.95 -53.14 64.07 -53.14 62.99 C -53.14 61.91 -52.26 61.04 -51.18 61.04 C -50.1 61.04 -49.22 61.91 -49.22 62.99 Z M -51.18 62.99"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -11.37 85.61 L -49.66 10.85"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -9.84 85.24 L -9.84 -16.34"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -8.32 85.61 L 29.97 10.85"></path></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>A fan triangulation of a hexagon.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is well-known. We include a proof for convenience. The strategy of the proof is illustrated for <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The number of triangulations of a convex <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon is given by the <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th Catalan number <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}=\frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}=\frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof can be done using an inductive argument. One checks that the claim is true for <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose an edge <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the triangle it is a part of. In an <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon, there are <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> other options for the third vertex of this triangle. All of these reduce the problem to one or two smaller cases, as to the left and right of this triangle, there are smaller polygons of size <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+4-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+4-m</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, for <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3,\dots,n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3,\dots,n+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
(For <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math>, there is only a polygon of size <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> on the right of the triangle, for <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n+2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is only a polygon of size <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> on the left of the triangle.) We count the number of triangulations of these two subpolygons and let <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> run:
This gives the total number of triangulations as <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n-1}+C_{1}C_{n-2}+...+C_{n-2}C_{1}+C_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n-1}+C_{1}C_{n-2}+...+C_{n-2}C_{1}+C_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a well-known recursive formula for the Catalan numbers.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-07-12-154547.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:1050/349;" width="1050" height="349" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Each possible triangulation of the octagon falls into one of these 6 types.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">There is a well known move on triangulated surfaces:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be a diagonal in the triangulation <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This defines a quadrilateral of the two triangles containing <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a new triangulation <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is obtained by replacing the diagonal <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> with the other diagonal of that quadrilateral. This local move is called a <em id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flip</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is a classical result that any two triangulation of a polygon can be linked by a sequence of flips (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hat91</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flip graph</em> of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph whose vertices are triangulations of the polygon, and two vertices <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected by an edge if and only if there exists a (single) flip linking <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Coloured triangulations, coloured flips</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we are interested in a generalisation of triangulations: we equip triangulations with a set of colours and define a new flip operation for them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> different colours. If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangulation of a polygon, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of its triangles (faces).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Colouring)<span id="S2.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangulation of a convex polygon.
By a <em id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">colouring</em> of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> we mean an assignment of colours from <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,m</annotation></semantics></math> for every triangle of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Coloured flip)<span id="S2.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of colours and <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangulation of a convex polygon with each triangle a colour in <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> be a diagonal incident with two triangles of the same colour <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <em id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-flip</em> of <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Replace <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by the flip of <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the underlying uncoloured triangulation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Change the colours of the two triangles incident with <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to the colour <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If the permutation <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a single cycle of the form <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2,\dots,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2,\dots,m)</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\mapsto i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\mapsto i+1</annotation></semantics></math>), we call a <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-flip simply an <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-coloured flip</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex polygon and let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of colours, let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation.
The <span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coloured flip graph</span> of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with colours <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and permutation <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span> or the
<math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-flip graph of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span>
is the graph whose vertices are the coloured triangulations of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and whose edges correspond to <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-flips.
We will often just call it the <span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flip graph</span> of the polygon.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The coloured triangulations are also counted in terms of Catalan numbers. We study the coloured flip graphs in this paper. We note that whenever no two adjacent triangles have the same colour, no edge can be flipped and we have an isolated vertex in the flip graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a convex <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math>-gon <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> colours.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There are </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}m^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}m^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> coloured triangulations of </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There are </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}m(m-1)^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}m(m-1)^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> triangulations of </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> where none of the diagonals can be flipped.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">. Any triangulation of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> triangles, so there are <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> different ways to colour a triangulation. The claim then follows from <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">. We consider the dual graph <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a given triangulation <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>: it has as vertices the triangles in <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and an edge between the two vertices of adjacent triangles. This graph is known to be a tree, it has <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and at least two leaves. We start by colouring one leaf with one of the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> colours and then proceed to colour adjacent vertices. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, for any new vertex we want to colour, there are <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> options. Hence the factor <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m-1)^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m-1)^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim then follows with <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Four-Colour Theorem</em> is one of the most famous mathematical problems in history. It concerns the question whether four colours are enough to colour any map drawn in the plane.
In 1977, Appel, Haken and Koch
established that four colours are enough (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AH77</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHK77</a>]</cite>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Four-Colour Theorem)<span id="S2.Thmthm10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any map on <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be colored using four colors such that any two regions sharing an edge are of different colours.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This result was proved with the assistance of computers. So far, there is no abstract proof of this theorem. The main motivation of this project is the search for an alternative
approach to its proof.
In 2002, Gravier and Payan showed that the Four-Colour Theorem is equivalent to a question on coloured flip graphs:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconj1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two arbitrary triangulations of a convex polygon <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> be two colours.
Then there exist colourings of <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and of <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that there is a sequence of <math id="Thmconj1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips between the two coloured triangulations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">GP02</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given any two triangulations of a convex polygon, it is possible to transform one into the other by a sequence of <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips if and only if
the Four-Colour Theorem holds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">So in order to give an abstract approach to the Four-Colour Theorem, it is enough to give an
abstract prove of <a href="#Thmconj1" title="Conjecture 1. ‣ 2.2. Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We will recall the proof of <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 2.11 ([]). ‣ 2.2. Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Connected components of coloured flip graphs</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first show that when studying coloured flip graphs, it is enough to consider <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is enough to determine <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-flip graphs for single cycle permutations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">If we have colours from multiple cycles, then we can divide the polygon up into smaller polygons, corresponding to areas with colours in the different cycles. These are invariant, as the cycle a colour comes from is invariant under a flip. Hence if we can get between permutations by using a colour permutation with multiple cycles, we can also get between them by using a single cycle with length the highest common multiple of all previous cycle lengths.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">So from now on we will assume that <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a single cycle of length <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. We now show that it is enough to consider <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math> depending on whether <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or even.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,2,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,2,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> colours. To determine whether two
triangulations <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are linked by an <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flip sequence, it is enough to consider the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured case if <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured case if <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we can assume <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a singe cycle of length <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>:
If <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then colour every triangle in <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> colour <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the first colour in the <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle. Note that after flipping the same quadrilateral <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> times, the colour will still be the same but the diagonal is flipped. So by finding a path of uncoloured flips between <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and replacing each move with <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> moves on the same diagonal, we have found a path between them which respects the permutation (after each set of <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> moves, the entire shape is still the same colour).
If <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is even, and we can find a 2-coloured flip sequence between <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can do so for any even cycle. Use the same colourings for <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and find a path between them as follows: if in the original path we are going from colour 1 to colour 2, perform one flip. If we are going from colour 2 to colour 1, replace the single flip with m-1 flips, as this will change the colour and diagonal as we wanted.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An example is given in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where the yellow to red flip is replaced by 3 flips, and the red to yellow flip is left as a single flip.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-07-21-102754.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/157;" width="333" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>A flip sequence with two colours, translated into a sequence with four colours 
<br class="ltx_break">(with <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=</annotation></semantics></math> (red, yellow, cyan, green)) </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will again consider an example of coloured hexagon <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> triangulations. In <a href="#S3.F4" title="In Example 3.3. ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we list all types of connected components of the coloured flip graph for <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-06-26-132816.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/96;" width="215" height="96" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-06-26-133430.png" id="S3.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/59;" width="215" height="59" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-06-26-135259.png" id="S3.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/108;" width="191" height="108" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-06-26-135715.png" id="S3.F4.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/101;" width="191" height="101" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-06-26-140312.png" id="S3.F4.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/207;" width="215" height="207" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Connected components of the coloured flip graph of <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> up to isometry, excluding the isolated points.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">From now on, we restrict to <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips, i.e. to the case <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>. We will often choose <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and indicate these colours by <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+,-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+,-</annotation></semantics></math> in the examples. We often use yellow and red as the two colours.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the following theorem, we need the notion of a flip sequence: let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured triangulation of a convex polygon, let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-coloured flip sequence</span> <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underline{\mu}=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mu}=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of 2-coloured flips where for <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=s,s-1,\dots,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=s,s-1,\dots,1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a diagonal in <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{i-1}\mu_{i-2}\cdots\mu_{s}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i-1}\mu_{i-2}\cdots\mu_{s}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> which can be 2-colour flipped and where <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-coloured flip in the coloured triangulation <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{i-1}\mu_{i-2}\cdots\mu_{s}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i-1}\mu_{i-2}\cdots\mu_{s}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote the 2-coloured triangulation obtained through this sequence by <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mu}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mu}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the coloured flip graph of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every connected component of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is either an isolated point or is of size <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangulation in a non-trivial connected component and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> a diagonal of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, then either <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be 2-coloured flipped or there exists a 2-coloured flip sequence
<math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underline{\mu}=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mu}=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>) such that <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\underline{\mu}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\underline{\mu}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be colour-flipped in <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mu}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mu}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangulation of <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a colouring.
We assume that there is at least one diagonal which can be 2-colour-flipped.
We mark the diagonals of <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with a blue or a red dot: A diagonal <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is marked blue if it can be flipped after some (possibly empty, if it can be flipped immediately) sequence of coloured flips. Diagonals of <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> which can never be flipped are marked with a red point.
By assumption, at least one diagonal is marked with a blue dot.
If there exists a diagonal with a red dot, find a triangle with with a red and a blue dot on two of its diagonals. Such a triangle always exists (see <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Remark 3.6. ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Remark</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>).
Call these two diagonals <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. The two triangles incident with <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> must be of different colours, since otherwise, we can flip it immediately.
We now execute a (coloured) flip sequence in order to flip the edge <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. At some point in this sequence (or at the end of the sequence), the colour of one of the triangles incident with edge <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has changed colour, and at this point, the edge <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> can be flipped.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is a contradiction, hence there can be no red dots and every edge can be flipped eventually. Hence there are at least <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices in this connected component of the coloured flip graph.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a triangulation <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> where at least one diagonal can be 2-colour flipped. We mark the diagonals of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by a blue dot if the diagonal can eventually be colour-flipped and with a red dot otherwise (as in the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>). Then there exists a triangle with a red and blue dot on two of its sides: We draw the dual graph <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> of the triangulation (as in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2.1. Coloured triangulations, coloured flips ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>):
it has vertices for the diagonals of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and edges between vertices <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever there is a triangle containing <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We equip this graph with the two colours. Since the graph <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, there has to be an edge between a red and a blue node.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following result shows that connected components of size <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> do exist in the coloured flip graph of <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a fan triangulation <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, with alternatingly coloured triangles apart from at one end. See <a href="#S3.F8" title="In 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for an illustration of such a fan triangulation of a decagon.
Then the connected component containing <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a single line with <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices: in every step, only a single 2-coloured flip can be made. Under this, the two triangles of the same colour move from one side of the fan to the other end of the fan.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-11-15-135622.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:134/128;" width="134" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>A coloured fan triangulation of a decagon.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the notion of a weighting on the vertices of a triangulated polygon with a <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">GP02</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">If we have a coloured triangulation and if <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangle of <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote its colour by
<math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\triangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\triangle)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that
<math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\triangle)\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\triangle)\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighting</span> of the polygon <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a choice of a triangulation <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and a function
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> assigning to each vertex of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> an element of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that there is a <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> where for every vertex <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=\sum_{x\in\triangle}s(\triangle)\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=\sum_{x\in\triangle}s(\triangle)\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math> (the sum is taken over all triangles incident with the vertex <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Two weightings of coloured triangulated quadrilaterals are shown in <a href="#S3.F7" title="In 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any two <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured triangulations which are in the same connected component of the flip graph have the same weighting.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be a weighting of a triangulated polygon with 2-colouring. If <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of the quadrilateral where the flip happens, the flip either changes two triangles with <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> to one triangle with <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> (or vice versa) or two triangles with <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> to one triangle with <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> (or vice versa). In all cases, <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> remains the same. (See <a href="#S3.F7" title="In 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">However, there exist 2-colourings which are not flip equivalent but have the same weighting, see Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for an example.</p>
</div>
