<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Example of quadrilateral valuation</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any cycle in the coloured flip graph is even.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a 2-coloured triangulation, let <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of triangles marked <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> in this triangulation. After every flip, <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> either increases or decreases by 2, hence every flip changes <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, if we reach <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> again after a sequence of coloured flips, this sequence has to have even length, since the number of triangles marked with a <math id="S3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> will be equal to <math id="S3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> again.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Structure of the flip graph</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show properties of the flip graph. In particular, we prove the existence of hypercubes in the flip graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two diagonals in a triangulation of a convex polygon. If two quadrilaterals share at most a diagonal, we say that the quadrilaterals are <span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">disjoint</span>. In this case, we say that the flips of <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and of <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a fan triangulation of <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> where each face is assigned the same colour (see Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for an example).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that we have <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> triangles in <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for a <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon. To maximise <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we start by choosing the first two triangles on the left in <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we keep choosing the quadrilateral close to the previous one sharing a common boundary. We end up with either all triangles been chosen or having one left. Hence, <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-07-21-114714.png" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:134/116;" width="134" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>A flip graph of the monocolour <math id="S3.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math>-gon, <math id="S3.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> contains 4 non-overlapping quadrilaterals, and hence a 4-dimensional hypercube subgraph.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a 2-coloured triangulation of a convex polygon.
Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the connected component of the flip graph containing <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that there are <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> diagonals in <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> which can be 2-coloured flipped and whose quadrilaterals are pairwise disjoint.
Then <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional hypercube.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> diagonals of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> which can be flipped independently by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i,j\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i,j\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, the flips <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> commute. We consider all the triangulations which can be reached from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by arbitrary <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-coloured flips of these <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> diagonals. In the subgraph of the flip graph they define, each of them has degree <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. So they form a subgraph isomorphic to a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional hypercube as claimed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_coroll">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-coloured flip graph of <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a connected component which is not planar.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the fan triangulation where every triangle is coloured with the same colour. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the connected component of the coloured flip which contains this coloured fan triangulation. Since <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a sub-polygon with the same structure as the fan decagon (see figure 8). Hence there are at least four quadrilaterals which can be flipped independently, given by the thick lines. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a 4-dimensional hypercube by <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Proposition 3.14. ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>. Denote this by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> has the complete bipartite graph <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> as a subgraph, and the latter is not planar, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be planar.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmnotex1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_note">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmnotex1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation</span></span><span id="Thmnotex1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmnotex1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmnotex1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two <math id="Thmnotex1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional hypercubes in a connected component of the flip graph to be <span id="Thmnotex1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">distinct</span> if they are disjoint or if their intersection is a union of hypercubes of smaller dimension.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> that is a fan triangulation of a convex <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon where all triangles have the same colour. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of the coloured flip graph. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is even, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains a </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dimensional hypercube and a </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{n}{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{n}{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dimensional hypercube;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is odd, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains at least two </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{n-1}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{n-1}{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dimensional hypercube.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When we assume <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to be even, the maximum number of independent coloured flips is <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we group pairs of adjacent faces starting with a face defined by a single diagonal. In a similar way, if we group adjacent faces starting from a face defined by two diagonals there are two possibilities. Either we end up with <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> independent flips, or we have <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math> independent flips.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to (i) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max k=\frac{n-1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max k=\frac{n-1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by removing either the first or the last triangle. Hence, we have at least two distinct hypercubes of dimension <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\frac{n-1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\frac{n-1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a version of <a href="#S3.Thmthm16" title="Lemma 3.16. ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> can be proved for more general triangulations: the number of disjoint quadrilaterals in an arbitrary 2-coloured triangulations gives a lower bound on the dimension of maximal dimensional hypercubes it contains. However, it is more difficult to determine the number of independent quadrilaterals, especially if there are inner triangles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.17</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate <a href="#S3.Thmthm16" title="Lemma 3.16. ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> for the uni-colored fan triangulation of <math id="S3.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\leq n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\leq n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> in Figures <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <a href="#S3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 3.1. Structure of the flip graph ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. In each figure from left to right, the first graph is the original triangulation, and then are the possible hypercubes of different dimensions, and the last one is the combination of all these hypercubes. We number the diagonals in <math id="S3.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and the numbers on edges of the <math id="S3.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional cube represents a flip of that diagonal. The black points represent the fan triangulation.</p>
</div>
</div>
