<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Observations and a conjecture</h2>

<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/Screenshot2023-07-03-153303.png" id="S4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/137;" width="381" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13. </span>A connected component of the flip graph for coloured nonagon triangulations which contains no fans, and has a minimum cycle size 20.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this paper by a number of observations and a conjecture. Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math>-gon.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmob1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ob">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmob1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 1</span></span><span id="Thmob1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmob1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmob1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="Thmob1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> any connected component of the 2-coloured flip graphs of <math id="Thmob1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a tree, or obtained from adding leaves onto a 4-cycle.
See <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Connected components of flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
For <math id="Thmob1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;4</annotation></semantics></math>, this is not true anymore. An example is a component for <math id="Thmob1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.F13" title="In 4. Observations and a conjecture ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmob2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ob">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmob2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 2</span></span><span id="Thmob2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmob2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmob2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are connected components of the flip graph which do not have any leaves, see e.g.<a href="#S4.F13" title="In 4. Observations and a conjecture ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> for <math id="Thmob2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> or <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Connected components of flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> in the case of <math id="Thmob2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/7-com.png" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/145;" width="238" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14. </span>A section of a connected component for the heptagon, which has 3 leaves of 2 different triangulation types.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the examples we considered, no two triangulations in a connected component contained two triangles with the same vertices but with different colours. See for example Figure <a href="#S4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 4. Observations and a conjecture ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> for an illustration. We suspect that this could be true in general:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span><span id="Thmconj2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In a connected component of the 2-coloured flip graph, a triangle cannot appear in the same position but with different colours.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 2.11 ([]). ‣ 2.2. Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the statement of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 2.11 ([]). ‣ 2.2. Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> from the Introduction: The Four-Colour Theorem holds if and only if for any two triangulations of a convex polygon, one can 2-colour them in a way that there exists a sequence of 2-coloured flips linking the two triangulations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This result by Gravier and Payan motivates the notion of coloured mutation. The work of Gravier and Payan has appeared in French in 2002. For the convenience of the reader, we summarize their reasoning in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first recall the notions needed.
In this section, we will use ‘signed triangulations’ to refer to 2-coloured triangulations in order to distinguishing from the notion of a colour in the 4-colour theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex polygon. We introduce the following definitions:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangulation of <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of all diagonals of <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> for its faces (the triangles).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sub-polygon</em> <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> is a polygon whose vertices are a subset of those of <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and which respects the cyclic order of the vertices of <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sub-polygon induced by all vertices except <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">signed triangulation</em> of <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-coloured triangulation <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the polygon, i.e. a pair <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,s\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:\mathcal{F}(T)\rightarrow\{+1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:\mathcal{F}(T)\rightarrow\{+1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-colouring of the triangles of <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the signed triangulation obtained from <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> by changing all signs. We write <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,s)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the class <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{T,s\},\{T,\overline{s}\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{T,s\},\{T,\overline{s}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="A1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">signed flip</span> is a 2-coloured flip of a diagonal of a signed triangulation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the definition of a weighting of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Definition 3.8. ‣ 3. Connected components of coloured flip graphs ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>) and introduce a notation suitable with the other terms of this section:
The pair <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,p\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:V(T)\to\{-1,0,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:V(T)\to\{-1,0,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function on the vertices of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (or of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>) is called a <span id="A1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighting</span> of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is there exists a 2-colouring <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every vertex <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=\sum_{t\in\mathcal{F}(T)}s(t)\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=\sum_{t\in\mathcal{F}(T)}s(t)\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly as before, if <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,p\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighting and <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> a 2-colouring giving rise to it, we write <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,\overline{p}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,\overline{p}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the weighting associated to <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,p)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the valuation <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, up to exchanging <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.I1.i6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="A1.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">valuation</em> of <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:\mathcal{D}(T)\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:\mathcal{D}(T)\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to every diagonal of <math id="A1.I1.i7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="A1.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">colouring</em> of <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,col)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,col)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col</annotation></semantics></math> is a 4-proper colouring of the vertices of <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., no two vertices adjacent under <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> share the same colour). We will often use the letters <math id="A1.I1.i8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate the four colours of a colouring. We only consider colourings up to permutation of colours.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,s\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a signed triangulation. The signs determine a weighting of <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by definition. There is a natural way to associate a valuation
<math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> to any signed triangulation <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,s\}</annotation></semantics></math> if a diagonal is incident with two triangles of the same sign, its valuation is set to be <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, its valuation is set to be <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, this procedure associates the same valuation <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,\overline{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,\overline{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. So we can naturally assign a valuation <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,s)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
