<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15. </span>A signed triangulation of heptagon with weighting, valuation, colouring.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that
“signed triangulations, weighting, valuation and colouring” are equivalent notions, up to taking the opposite signs/weights:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,s)\equiv(T,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,s)\equiv(T,p)</annotation></semantics></math>: Weightings and 2-colourings are equivalent by definition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,s)\equiv(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,s)\equiv(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math>:
Any signed triangulation <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,s)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a valuation <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> as we have explained above (for any diagonal <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(xy)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(xy)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the two triangles adjacent to <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> have the same sign). Conversely, any valuation <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to two signed triangulations <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,s\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T,\overline{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T,\overline{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\equiv(T,col)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\equiv(T,col)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a valuation <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a 4-colouring <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col</annotation></semantics></math> of the vertices of <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math>: Choose a vertex of degree 2 in <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a vertex lies in a triangle which has two boundary edges (every triangulation has at least two such triangles).
We colour the three vertices of this triangles in three different colours. We proceed as follows: for any quadrilateral with vertices <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xyzt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xyzt</annotation></semantics></math>, formed by two adjacent triangles sharing the common diagonal <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yt</annotation></semantics></math>, we colour <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,z</annotation></semantics></math> in the same colour if and only if the diagonal <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yt</annotation></semantics></math> is valued 1 under <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Starting with the above triangle, we thus obtain a colouring of <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with (up to) four colours. The colouring <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to permutation of the colours.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Reciprocally, starting from <math id="A1.I2.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,col)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,col)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a valuation of <math id="A1.I2.i3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by setting a diagonal of any quadrilateral to be 0 if and only if the four vertices of the quadrilateral this diagonal determines are all coloured differently.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By the above, it makes sense to write <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s,p,v,col\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s,p,v,col\}</annotation></semantics></math> as these are all equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangulation of a polygon. We comment on the effect of a flip on the notions weighting, valuation and colouring. See <a href="#A1.F16" title="In Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> for an illustration.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any flippable diagonal has valuation <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. If we flip it, the new diagonal also has valuation <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> while the diagonals bounding the corresponding quadrilateral change their valuation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The weighting of the vertices remains unchanged under flips.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any colouring for <math id="A1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is still a colouring for the new triangulation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
