nObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -23.14 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="A1.F16.pic6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.23 -0.52)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="A1.F16.pic6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -23.14 -33.99)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="A1.F16.pic6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 16. </span>The effect of a signed flip on weighting, valuation, colouring.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a 3-colour colouring of a triangulation corresponds to the case where each diagonal has value 1, and such signed triangulation is called <em id="A1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alternating</em>.
Alternating signed triangulations are isolated vertices in the flip exchange graph and so they are not of interest for us.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.4</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be two signed triangulations of the same polygon. We write <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)\sim(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)\sim(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a sequence of 2-coloured flips from <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This sequence may be empty (i.e. we allow <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\varepsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\varepsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>).
One can check that <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>
is an equivalence relation, we denote the class of <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A1.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T,\varepsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T,\varepsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 2.11 ([]). ‣ 2.2. Triangulations of polygons and the Four-Colour Theorem ‣ 2. Background ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> which we reformulate as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.5</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\neq(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\neq(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be signed triangulations of <math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="A1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and it uses 4 colours.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem A.5. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using Remark <a href="#A1.Thmthm3" title="Remark A.3. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> one can see that a coloured flip does not change the colouring of the vertices. Iterating, we get that
<math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> implies
<math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since we assumed that the two triangulations are different, the sequence of signed flips needed to go from <math id="A1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty, i.e. the flip graph is not a single point and there is at least one diagonal valued with <math id="A1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> uses four colours.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">To prove the converse of the theorem, we first show three lemmas. We have to study the vertices of <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and their neighbours. In a triangulated polygon any vertex of <math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has neighbours on the boundary and potentially neighbours through diagonals of the triangulation. When dealing with the former, we refer to them as neighbours along the boundary (or on the boundary).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.6</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> be a signed triangulation of a polygon <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> a vertex of <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the two neighbours of <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> along the boundary are the only two neighbours of <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with the same colour.
Then <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has 3 or 4 neighbours, and <math id="A1.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, <math id="A1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have only 2 neighbours in this case as in that case, these would belong to a common triangle with <math id="A1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose for contradiction that the vertex <math id="A1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has at least five neighbours. Then the two neighbours on the polygon are not the only two neighbours of the same colour in <math id="A1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>: We can only colour three neighbours of <math id="A1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with distinct colours (different from the colour of <math id="A1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>). And we would have at least three vertices of the same colour or another pair of neighbours with the same colour. Hence <math id="A1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have more than 4 neighbours.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">In case <math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has three neighbours, these four vertices span a quadrilateral (with <math id="A1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>) and the diagonal ending at <math id="A1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has value <math id="A1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> as the other end must be of a different colour. In particular, the two triangles incident with <math id="A1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> have opposite sign and <math id="A1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has weight <math id="A1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">In case <math id="A1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has four neighbours, the two neighbours which are linked to <math id="A1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by diagonals must be of two different colours which are also different from the colour of <math id="A1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, both these diagonals have value <math id="A1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is incident with three triangles of the same sign and the weight <math id="A1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (mod <math id="A1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.7</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> be a signed triangulation of <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> a vertex of <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has no two neighbours of the same colour, then <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has 2 or 3 neighbours and the weight <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="A1.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that <math id="A1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> can only have 2 or 3 neighbours as if there are more, there would be at least two of them with the same colour.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">In case <math id="A1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has only two neighours, it is incdent with only one triangle and so <math id="A1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (mod <math id="A1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">So assume that <math id="A1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has three neighbours. In the quadrilateral spanned by <math id="A1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its three neighbours, <math id="A1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has a diagonal connecting <math id="A1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with the fourth vertex, say <math id="A1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The vertices <math id="A1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> have to be of different colour and so all four vertices of this quadrilateral are of different colours. Hence the diagonal <math id="A1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> has value <math id="A1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. So the two triangles at <math id="A1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> are of the same sign and the weight <math id="A1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="A1.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A1.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mod 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mod 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.8</span></span><span id="A1.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> be a signed triangulation of a polygon <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be a vertex of <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if its two neighbours on <math id="A1.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> have the same colour.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is enough to consider the full subgraph of the triangulated polygon induced by <math id="A1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (it consists of <math id="A1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, of all vertices connected with <math id="A1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and of all boundary edges and diagonals connecting them).
The idea is to use induction on the degree of the vertex <math id="A1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">(1) If <math id="A1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has no two neighbours of the same colour, then <math id="A1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm7" title="Lemma A.7. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">(2) If the two neighbours of <math id="A1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> on the polygon are the only neighbours of <math id="A1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with the same colour, then <math id="A1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm6" title="Lemma A.6. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.
With (1) and (2) we have covered all cases where <math id="A1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (in degree <math id="A1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, if there are vertices of the same colour among the neighbours of <math id="A1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, they have to be on the boundary, for a colouring to be valid).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">So the result holds for vertices <math id="A1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="A1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">(3) It remains to check the general situation. Let <math id="A1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the neighbours of <math id="A1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> being along the boundary and where <math id="A1.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. See left hand picture of Figure <a href="#A1.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, there are vertices among the <math id="A1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the same colour. Pick <math id="A1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j-1</annotation></semantics></math> of the same colour such that there are no two vertices of the same colour among <math id="A1.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i+1},\dots,y_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i+1},\dots,y_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the triangulated subpolygon on the vertices <math id="A1.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y_{i},y_{i+1},\dots,y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y_{i},y_{i+1},\dots,y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the same argument as in Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm6" title="Lemma A.6. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>, we find that either <math id="A1.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=i+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=i+2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=i+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=i+3</annotation></semantics></math> and that the triangles incident with <math id="A1.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="A1.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> in this subpolygon (there are either two triangles of opposite signs or three triangles of the same sign).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">We then identify <math id="A1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, getting a new polygon <math id="A1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing the degree of <math id="A1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in it, see right hand side of Figure <a href="#A1.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>. So in the polygon <math id="A1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the weight of <math id="A1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the two neigbhours <math id="A1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> on the boundary have the same colour.
Since the region between <math id="A1.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes by <math id="A1.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to the weight, the claim holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F17" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/general.png" id="A1.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:250/139;" width="250" height="139" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2402.06546v1/Figures/general-reduced.png" id="A1.F17.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:184/141;" width="184" height="141" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 17. </span>The neighbourhood of <math id="A1.F17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.F17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="A1.F17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <math id="A1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longleftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟸</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longleftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem A.5. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that there exists a polygon <math id="A1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and two triangulations <math id="A1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime}v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime}v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> which provide a counterexample. Let <math id="A1.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be minimal with this property. The polygon <math id="A1.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has at least <math id="A1.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> vertices (one can check that the theorem is true for <math id="A1.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices). So <math id="A1.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T,v)=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, this
colouring uses all four colours, and there is no sequence of signed flips between these two signed triangulations.
Among the vertices of <math id="A1.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of degree 2 we choose a vertex <math id="A1.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that <math id="A1.p27.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-x</annotation></semantics></math> (the triangulated polygon without the triangle at <math id="A1.p27.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>) is still coloured with four colours. Such a vertex always exists as <math id="A1.p27.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has at least <math id="A1.p27.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and among them at least two vertices of degree <math id="A1.p27.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. At least one of them satisfies this condition (if one removes a degree 2 vertex <math id="A1.p27.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining colouring only uses <math id="A1.p27.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> colours, one replaces <math id="A1.p27.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> by another degree <math id="A1.p27.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertex in <math id="A1.p27.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>).
Since all four colours are present in <math id="A1.p27.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-x</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a diagonal with valuation <math id="A1.p27.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">If there exists a signed triangulation <math id="A1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the equivalence class <math id="A1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, then by minimality of the size of <math id="A1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that for the polygon <math id="A1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P-x</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A1.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T-x,v)\sim(T^{\prime\prime}-x,v^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T-x,v)\sim(T^{\prime\prime}-x,v^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. But then
<math id="A1.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and the latter is in the equivalence class of <math id="A1.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A1.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)\sim(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">So we can assume that <math id="A1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> in every triangulation in <math id="A1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">We partition this equivalence class into two sets <math id="A1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will show that these are both empty, thus proving that no counter-example to the implication <math id="A1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> exists.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">We define <math id="A1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of all signed triangulations in <math id="A1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> having a diagonal of value <math id="A1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> incident with <math id="A1.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="A1.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the ones where every diagonal at <math id="A1.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has value <math id="A1.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. These are the signed triangulations which are alternating on the subpolygon induced by <math id="A1.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and all its neighbours in <math id="A1.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Since the degree of <math id="A1.p31.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.p31.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> for any signed triangulation in <math id="A1.p31.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is always at least one diagonal at <math id="A1.p31.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Claim: <math id="A1.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">From the elements of <math id="A1.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> choose a signed triangulation <math id="A1.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal degree (this degree is <math id="A1.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> as we have seen). The two neighbours of <math id="A1.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (along the boundary of the polygon) are adjacent in <math id="A1.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="A1.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p32.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) and so have different colour. By Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmthm8" title="Lemma A.8. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>, this means that <math id="A1.p32.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime\prime}(x)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime\prime}(x)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p32.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weighting of <math id="A1.p32.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. This weighting is the same as that of <math id="A1.p32.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and as that of <math id="A1.p32.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,v)</annotation></semantics></math> as their colourings are the same.
If there is a diagonal of value <math id="A1.p32.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> incident with <math id="A1.p32.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="A1.p32.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (for some <math id="A1.p32.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>), we flip a diagonal with value <math id="A1.p32.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> next to this diagonal. Then the diagonal <math id="A1.p32.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has value <math id="A1.p32.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this new triangulation, the degree of <math id="A1.p32.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has gone down by one and we reach a contradiction.
So all diagonals at <math id="A1.p32.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> must have value <math id="A1.p32.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We flip the first such diagonal at <math id="A1.p32.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. going clockwise through these diagonals). The result is a triangulation where either <math id="A1.p32.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p32.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (contradicting that the vertex <math id="A1.p32.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="A1.p32.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> for all elements of <math id="A1.p32.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T^{\prime},v^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>) or it has degree <math id="A1.p32.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and no diagonal of value <math id="A1.p32.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> incident with it, implying that <math id="A1.p32.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime\prime}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime\prime}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> (a contradiction to <math id="A1.p32.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime\prime}(x)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime\prime}(x)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>) or the resulting triangulation is an element of <math id="A1.p32.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p32.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has smaller degree.
Figure <a href="#A1.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> illustrates the first two of these cases.
In all three cases, this leads to a contradiction.
Therefore, <math id="A1.p32.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Claim: <math id="A1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty:</span>
<br class="ltx_break">Recall that the signed triangulation of the subpolygon induced by <math id="A1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its neighbours in <math id="A1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is alternating (all diagonals at <math id="A1.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> have value <math id="A1.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>). For any <math id="A1.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> an element of <math id="A1.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="A1.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> the maximal alternating subpolygon (maximal by inclusion) which contains <math id="A1.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its neighbours.
Let <math id="A1.p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of <math id="A1.p33.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which minimizes the size of <math id="A1.p33.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="A1.p33.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal as alternating signed polygon, the boundary edges of <math id="A1.p33.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> which are diagonals in the original triangulated polygon have to have value <math id="A1.p33.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="A1.p33.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="col(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">col(T^{\prime\prime},v^{\prime\prime})=col(T^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and all four colours appear, there exists at least one diagonal of value <math id="A1.p33.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (so such a boundary edge of <math id="A1.p33.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> has to exist).
If we flip this diagonal, we obtain a new triangulation <math id="A1.p33.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
If this diagonal is incident with two edges (two diagonals or one diagonal and a boundary edge) at <math id="A1.p33.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p33.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="A1.p33.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#A1.F19" title="Figure 19 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> for an illustration). However, <math id="A1.p33.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Otherwise, <math id="A1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="A1.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(S)</annotation></semantics></math> smaller than <math id="A1.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#A1.F20" title="Figure 20 ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Flip graphs of coloured triangulations of convex polygons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> for an illustration), also a contradiction.
So <math id="A1.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.
∎</span></p>
